---
/**
 * /learn/what-is-surplus: canonical answer page for surplus and who keeps
 * it. The distribution facts mirror /pricing and llms.txt verbatim in
 * substance; the sovereign list comes from the shared chain data.
 */
import ContentPage from '../../layouts/ContentPage.astro'
import { canonicalPage } from '../../data/canonicalPages'
import { SOVEREIGN_CHAIN_LIST as sovereignList } from '../../data/chains'

const { updated } = canonicalPage('/learn/what-is-surplus/')

const FAQS = [
  {
    q: 'What is surplus?',
    a: `Surplus is execution beyond the terms you signed: your order named a limit, settlement achieved better, and the difference is surplus. On a sell order it means receiving more of the buy token than your floor required; on a buy order, spending less of the sell token than your cap allowed.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Where does surplus come from?',
    a: `Three main places. Prices move between signing and settlement, and movement in your favor lands beyond your limit. Solvers compete, so a better route, a market-maker quote, or private inventory found by the winning solver shows up as execution above your floor. And a coincidence of wants inside the batch skips pool fees and price impact entirely, with the saving surfacing as surplus on both sides.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Who keeps the surplus?',
    a: `On ${sovereignList}, Ophis retains 80% of reference-quote improvement on volatile pairs, capped at 30 bps of volume, or 50% on stable pairs, capped at 10 bps; the trader receives the remainder. CoW-hosted chains follow CoW Protocol's upstream surplus policy.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'How is surplus different from positive slippage?',
    a: `Positive slippage is the tolerance-model cousin: price movement in your favor inside a router swap. Whether it reaches you depends on how the router handles it, and captured positive slippage has historically been a quiet revenue line in parts of the industry. Surplus in a batch auction is explicit by construction: the signed limit defines exactly where your terms end, so everything beyond it is measurable, attributable, and returned under published rules.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I see the surplus on my trades?',
    a: `Yes. Every settlement is public, and the executed price against your signed limit is checkable on-chain per order. The Ophis explorer shows settlements, and the swap app surfaces the improvement on an order once it fills.`,
  },
]
---

<ContentPage
  title="What is surplus, and who keeps it? | Ophis"
  description="Surplus is execution beyond your signed limit: where it comes from (price movement, solver competition, peer matches) and who keeps it on each chain."
  path="/learn/what-is-surplus/"
  heading="What is surplus, and who keeps it?"
  lede="Your signed limit defines where your terms end. Everything better than that is surplus, and the rules for who keeps it are published per chain."
  updated={updated}
  faqs={FAQS}
>
  <p>
    <strong>The short version:</strong> surplus is the gap between what you signed for and the
    better execution settlement achieved. It comes from price movement, solver competition, and
    peer matches. On Ophis-operated chains, the trader receives the remainder after Ophis's capped
    reference-improvement capture, while CoW-hosted chains pass through CoW Protocol's upstream
    surplus policy.
  </p>

  {FAQS.map((f) => (
    <section>
      <h2>{f.q}</h2>
      <p>{f.a}</p>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>Go deeper</h2>
  <p>
